# Line endings for the evidence corpus.
#
# The body hashes in research/raw/.fetches.jsonl were computed over the bytes the
# collector fetched, which end in LF. On a default Windows checkout
# (core.autocrlf=true) git's smudge filter rewrites every text file it checks out
# to CRLF, so every one of those hashes stops recomputing: verifyLedger reports
# all six captures as body-unmodified, handoff and preflight exit with blockers,
# and the two-machine workflow is unusable on a default Windows builder.
#
# Nothing was lost and nothing needs re-collecting - the files travelled fine and
# git rewrote them locally on the way out of the object store. Pinning them here
# makes the checkout write what the ledger hashed, on every platform, regardless
# of the reader's core.autocrlf. This file outranks that setting.
#
# See ADR-0020 and fetchproof/lib/handoff.mjs, which detects this exact case
# and says so instead of blaming a missing push.
research/raw/*   text eol=lf
*.jsonl          text eol=lf
research/*.md    text eol=lf

# And the same reasoning, applied to the rest of the repository rather than only to the
# part whose hashes made the problem visible. Without this, every other text file still
# follows the reader's core.autocrlf: `git add` warns on ~200 files, and a checkout on a
# second machine differs byte-for-byte from this one for no reason anybody chose.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The commit gate is a POSIX sh script. CRLF in a shebang script is a syntax error
# on every POSIX host, and the failure is a hook that cannot run - which, per
# ADR-0001, git reports as a clean commit.
fetchproof/githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf
